 dict(slug='industrial-underwater-inspection',no='01',verb='INSPECT',title='Industrial underwater inspection',headline='Make submerged condition visible.',lead='Choose an ROV around the inspection result you need, then work back through visibility, access, depth and sensor requirements.',keyword='industrial inspection ROV',products=['r-30','r-45'],img='r-45.png',buyers='Inspection contractors, water utilities and hydropower maintenance teams.',sections=[('Start with the inspection objective','Documenting general condition, locating a defect and measuring its extent are different tasks. Define the structure, required coverage, reporting format and acceptance criteria before comparing vehicles.'),('Let visibility guide the sensing package','An optical camera provides interpretable detail when water clarity and lighting permit. Imaging sonar may help navigation and feature recognition in poor visibility. Neither automatically supplies survey-grade dimensions or a complete defect assessment.'),('Match the vehicle to the site','Compare working depth, currents, smallest access opening, vehicle clearance and deployment space. The R-30 and R-45 have different size and payload allowances. Evaluate them with the actual sensors installed.'),('Plan the complete field system','Account for the control station, tether, reel, power supply, launch method and recovery equipment. A vehicle specification alone does not describe the staffing, transport or infrastructure needed for a mission.'),('Define usable outputs','Specify video recording, time references, position references and annotations. Confirm which files can be exported and how observations will be connected to the asset owner’s reporting workflow.')],inputs=['Structure drawings and inspection objectives','Depth, current and expected visibility','Access dimensions and launch location','Required sensors, data formats and reporting criteria'],faq=[('Which ROV should I choose?','Start with the payload, access and site conditions. The R-30 has a brochure payload of 3 kg; the R-45 has 8 kg. The complete integrated package still needs review.'),('Can it inspect without visibility?','A suitable sonar can provide useful information in low visibility, but it is a separate sensing choice. Define what the mission must detect and verify that the proposed package can do it.')]),
 dict(slug='underwater-tunnel-inspection',no='02',verb='REACH',title='Long-distance underwater tunnel inspection',headline='A long tunnel needs a complete mission plan.',lead='Inspection of flooded water tunnels depends on navigation, tether control and recovery as much as the vehicle’s rated range.',keyword='underwater tunnel inspection ROV',products=['ad-400'],img='ad-400.jpg',buyers='Hydropower operators, water-conveyance asset owners and specialist inspection contractors.',sections=[('Map the route before selecting the system','Provide the tunnel length, section, gradient, bends, constrictions and known obstructions. A straight test route cannot establish performance through an arbitrary tunnel geometry.'),('Engineer power, communications and tether handling','The AD-400 uses an external tethered system. Review tether storage, deployment, bend contact, drag and retrieval. Verify how the complete package maintains power and communication over the intended route.'),('Tie findings to a position reference','Agree how inspection distance and location will be referenced, what uncertainty is acceptable and which instruments support it. Request actual data samples rather than relying on a positioning feature name.'),('Agree an abnormal-event recovery plan','Document the response to power or communication loss, snagging, sediment obstruction and unexpected flow. Define the retrieval equipment, access arrangements and site responsibilities.'),('Verify range against the actual mission','The official AD-400 page lists a maximum operation distance of 5 km; the supplied brochure lists 5.5 km. Neither figure is a universal guarantee. Confirm the supplied version, conditions and project acceptance test.')],inputs=['Tunnel route, cross-sections and bends','Water depth, flow and operating restrictions','Entry point, deployment area and isolation arrangements','Required defect coverage, location reference and recovery plan'],faq=[('Does this apply to every penstock?','No. Pressure conduits differ in geometry, flow and access. Treat each penstock as a separate engineering review rather than assuming tunnel capability transfers.'),('Can inspection proceed without shutdown?','Operational access depends on flow, isolation, permits and the owner’s procedures. A shutdown-free mission should only be promised after site-specific review.')]),
